@@@ note
As sentinel-byte-in-text, but arriving through a code span rather than plain text — the byte
reaches the rendered string through several node types, which is why it is removed from the
input at the door rather than sanitised per literal on the way out. See #9.

@@@ input
a `xy` b

@@@ wrap 80
a `xy` b

@@@ unwrap
a `xy` b

@@@ html-drift
The sentinel byte is dropped rather than preserved, so the rendered output loses it. That is
the intended trade: U+0001 carries no meaning in CommonMark, and keeping it would leave the
in-band hard-break marker ambiguous.
